.TH INSTALLERD 8 "October 2026" "Peios" "System Administration"
.SH NAME
installerd \- Peios installation and repair service
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B installerd
.RB [ --socket " " PATH ]
.RB [ --medium " " PATH ]
.RB [ --force ]
.br
.B installerd --dry-run
.RB [ --socket " " PATH ]
.RB [ --inventory " " FILE ]
.RB [ --fail-at " " PHASE ]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B installerd
owns the privileged installation, repair, and upgrade flow.  It exposes that
flow over MSIP; presentation programs such as
.BR install-tui (1)
connect to its Unix-domain socket and hold no disk-management privilege.
.PP
The service normally reads the pristine system image from the mounted
installation medium, writes the selected disk, exchanges live-boot for
disk-boot in the target package database, stages installed-system registry
seeds, and regenerates the initramfs and UKI.
.PP
An installation also carries GXWI's key and certificate,
.IR /var/state/gxwi ,
from the medium into the installed system, descriptors and all, so a browser
that installed the machine goes on trusting it and first-boot setup continues
in the same tab.  Every installation does, whichever surface started it.  A
medium without them, or a copy that fails, leaves the installed system to make
its own when GXWI first starts there; the installation goes on either way.
An upgrade carries nothing: the system's own key stays.
.PP
The first page says where the machine is in a browser and its certificate's
SHA-256 fingerprint, read from
.I /var/state/gxwi/certificate.sha256
as the page is made, so that someone at the console can check what a browser
shows before going past its warning.  Nothing is said while there is no
certificate.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI --socket " PATH"
Listen on PATH instead of
.IR /run/installerd.sock .
.TP
.BI --medium " PATH"
Use PATH as the mounted installation medium instead of
.IR /media/peios .
.TP
.B --force
Allow the partitioning tool to replace a table it did not create.  This never
suppresses the mounted-device guard or the confirmation in the MSIP flow.
.TP
.B --dry-run
Exercise the complete conversation without changing disks.  The machine's
disks and their partitions are listed as they are; what is on them is not
looked into, which takes a mount.
.TP
.BI --inventory " FILE"
With
.BR --dry-run ,
pretend to be the machine the JSON in FILE describes instead of probing this
one: its
.I disks
and what each holds, the storage
.I controllers
nothing drives, and the release its
.I medium
carries.  For showing a surface a machine other than the one it is being
worked on.  A key that is not recognised is an error.
.TP
.BI --fail-at " PHASE"
With
.BR --dry-run ,
fail every job part way through PHASE, which is named as the end of the
phase's element ref:
.BR partition ,
.BR format ,
.B copy
or
.B boot
for an installation.  For showing a surface a job that goes wrong.
.TP
.BR -h , " --help"
Show command help.
.TP
.BR -V , " --version"
Show the program version.
.SH SECURITY
The daemon is intended to run as SYSTEM.  Its socket descriptor grants access
only to SYSTEM and Administrators.  The terminal surface is deliberately a
separate process.
.SH FILES
.TP
.I /run/installerd.sock
Default MSIP socket.
.TP
.I /media/peios/rootfs.squashfs
Default pristine system image.
.TP
.I /var/state/gxwi
GXWI's key and certificate, carried into an installed system; see
.BR gxwid (1).
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR gxwid (1),
.BR install-tui (1),
.BR msip-drive (1),
.BR peipkg (8)
